cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PatchCraft: image patch extraction, reconstruction, pairing and seam-aware stitching"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Leonardo
    family-names: Marques de Souza
    email: leonardo.marques.souza@gmail.com
version: "0.5.4"
date-released: "2026-09-04"
repository-code: "https://github.com/LeoPR/PatchCraft"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  PatchCraft cuts one image into patches and puts it back. One call takes one
  (C, H, W) float tensor and returns one tensor: there is no batch axis, no
  dataset, no dataloader and no model, and that boundary is documented as
  binding rather than provisional. It owns the unfold and fold arithmetic, the
  geometry validation and the seam blending, so a pipeline can own the rest.

  Two silent defects motivate it. The intuitive reshape of F.unfold's output
  gives the right shape with the wrong pixels, and a stride that does not cover
  the image leaves pixels at zero without any error: on a 128 by 128 image with
  patch 32 and stride 20, 3840 of 16384 pixels. Both are checked once here,
  with tests around them.

  The numeric contract is stated as a caller-evaluable predicate rather than a
  promise. The extract and reconstruct round trip is bit-exact if and only if
  every value of the overlap coverage map is a power of two, always true at
  stride equal to patch size, and outside that rule the per-pixel error is
  bounded by (k+1) times eps times the absolute value, with k the pixel's
  coverage count. An earlier and weaker form of that claim was published, then
  measured false and publicly retracted; a falsification suite now enumerates
  the 126,736 legal geometries of the space independently of the predicate and
  tries to break both halves of it.

  Five of the six published wheels carry a Rust kernel for the overlapping
  fold, compiled into the wheel itself rather than into a separate package, and
  bit-identical to the pure-torch path by test. Every other platform installs a
  universal wheel and runs the same operation in torch.

  Pre-1.0: output values may change when the middle digit moves, and the
  changelog records each change with the measurement behind it.
keywords:
  - image-processing
  - patches
  - super-resolution
  - pytorch
  - tiling
  - reconstruction
  - seam-blending
  - numerical-reproducibility
